WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.210
The earliest tools for roasting coffee beans
were pans you would hold over hot coals or an

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:06.240
open fire.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:12.380
But today, coffee roasting is a far more
sophisticated process using large, often

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:17.840
fully automated machines with built in gas
burners that heat air to roast the beans.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:25.120
This coffee roaster is both programmable and
manually adjustable by touch panel.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:30.630
The roastmaster can tailor the temperature
and roasting time to influence flavor,

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:37.440
acidity and other characteristics of coffee
to shape the roast chamber workers feed a

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.110
sheet of stainless steel through a sheet
metal roll.

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:45.510
The roast chamber is the drum in which the
beans roast by convection heat.

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:50.750
The next step is to weld the rolled sheet
into a cylinder.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:56.630
Then workers grind and polish the welds
until they're flat, smooth and shiny.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:02.420
Another worker makes the cooling tray in a
similar way, but with a support band welded

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:07.580
to the top. The cooling tray is the drum
into which the hot beans drop when they exit

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:08.730
the rose chamber.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:09.920
Stirring arms.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.720
Circulate the beans as a fan draws air
through the tray to cool the hot beans.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:19.010
Certain parts of the roaster are cut from a
stainless steel sheet.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.800
Stainless steel is the ideal material, not
only because it's stylish, but also because

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.260
it's durable and corrosion resistant.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:32.820
This computer guided laser cutter is slicing
out a safety component called the heat

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:37.860
shield, which prevents the roastmaster from
accidentally touching a hot surface.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:46.040
The heat shield, like many other parts cut
from stainless steel sheets, has to be formed

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:47.870
to a very precise shape.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.490
A worker bends angles and curves into the
metal with a press brake.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:58.730
Another component the Trier lets you draw a
sample of beans during roasting.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:05.200
A craftsman, constructs the Trier by welding
various smaller parts to a piece of stainless

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:11.260
steel tube, then meticulously grinding and
polishing the welds until they're smooth.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:17.750
This high pressure water Jet cutter also
cuts parts from stainless steel sheets.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:23.080
This component is one of six flights, which
is part of the paddle that lifts and mixes

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:26.770
the beans inside the roast chamber so that
they roast evenly.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.350
To form the flights to the required shape.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.810
A worker curves them one at a time in a
press.

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:47.510
Next, the welder places all the paddle
components into a specialized fixture which

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:49.250
positions them correctly.

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:52.310
First, he aligns the spokes to the paddle
shaft.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.610
Then he welds the parts in place.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.020
He positions the flights, clamps them
securely and welds them on.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:14.090
The combination of inner and outer flights
lifts the beans into the airflow, ensuring

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:15.740
the beans roast evenly.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.840
A custom designed, grinding machine hones
the flights to produce a small clearance of

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:27.550
around a 10th of an inch between the edge of
the paddle and the wall of the roast chamber.

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:33.220
This design ensures the paddle is wide
enough to pick up every last coffee bean

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.740
without touching the wall while rotating.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:45.960
Once they install the paddle, a worker
closes up the rose chamber with a face plate.

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:52.790
Alignment pins, ensure the face plate is
properly positioned.

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:57.440
The paddle shaft protrudes through a bearing
in the face plate.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:04.690
The assembly team uses a hoist to lift the
heavy chamber and position it on top of the

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:06.730
coffee roaster stainless steel support
frame.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:13.870
They install the heat shield that was cut by
the laser cutter and bent to shape in the

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:23.230
press break. On top, the assembly team
mounts the machine's funnel shaped hopper.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:27.700
The hopper feeds the unroasted coffee beans
to the roast chamber below.

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:34.620
The Hopper's lid has a tube which connects
to a vacuum system that draws the coffee

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:36.570
beans up through a plastic hose.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:44.310
On the front of the roasting chamber, an
assembler hangs a hinged discharge door that

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:45.750
has a viewing window.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.620
He plugs in a sensor that measures the
temperature of the beans in the roasting

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:53.490
chamber and sends that information to the
machine's computer.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:58.960
This coffee roasting machine has three
additional temperature sensors that send data

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:00.070
to the computer.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:05.170
These sensors measure the temperature of the
air entering and exiting the roast chamber

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:09.490
and the temperature of the clean air
exhausting from the machine after the burner,

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:12.940
incinerates the smoke generated by the
roasting process.

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:19.700
Workers continue assembly by installing the
cooling tray beneath the discharge door of

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:20.930
the roasting chamber.

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:25.910
The cooling tray sits on a support frame
which contains a motor that turns the

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:27.980
stirring arms inside the tray.

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:33.800
At the back of the machine, a worker
installs the trays, cooling fan and the motor

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:34.850
that drives it.

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:41.270
This cage measures the pressure of the air
that mixes with the gas going into the

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:42.440
machine's burner.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:51.820
Next they installed the circulation fan.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:56.620
The fan is designed to withstand high heat
and move air efficiently throughout the

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:57.700
coffee roaster.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:06.340
The bottom end of the stainless steel tube
connects to a vacuum motor.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:11.680
The top end connects to the tube protruding
from the feed hoppers lid.

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:16.960
To fill the roasting chamber, the vacuum
motor sucks the raw green coffee beans up

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:22.810
into the hopper, which drops the beans into
the chamber, where as they roast their papery

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:25.150
skin called, the chaff breaks off.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:31.800
This separating device known as a cyclone
connects to the circulation fan.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:36.630
The fan blows hot air returning from the
roasting chamber into the cyclone at high

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:42.090
velocity. The air circulates in a downward
spiral past the burner at the base of the

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:44.460
cyclone, incinerating the smoke.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:51.180
This also draws the chaff out of the air
into a barrel nozzle spray water on the chaff

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:55.590
to prevent it from catching fire, which is a
common hazard with traditional coffee

00:06:55.590 --> 00:07:03.190
roasters. The circulation fan then forces
the clean hot air up and out the top of the

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:08.530
cyclone through this S-shaped insulated air
duct back into the roasting chamber.

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:16.910
A technician in the factory's electrical
department, assembles the machine's control

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:19.440
panel, among other components.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:24.740
The circuitry runs control systems and the
machine's six electric motors, which operate

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:31.190
moving parts such as the beam drawing vacuum
motor, the circulation fan, the cooling tray

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:34.010
fan, the roasting chamber paddle and cooling
tray.

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:35.480
Stirring arms.

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:45.480
Workers then install the control panel in a
cabinet on the side of the machine.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:52.500
They connect all the wires.

00:07:56.350 --> 00:08:00.520
They mount the machine's computer adjacent
to the control panel cabinet.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:05.000
Next, a worker assembles the cooling tray
system.

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:08.090
A motor under the tray drives the stirring
arms.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:13.700
The hot roasted coffee beans sit on top of
the stainless steel screen.

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:19.130
The cooling fan pulls ambient air through
the beans, down through the openings of the

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:21.530
screen, then out an exhaust pipe to the
outdoors.

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:29.300
The stirring arms, move the beans around so
that they cool quickly and evenly, which

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.910
prevents the beans from continuing to roast.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:40.790
The factory runs every finished coffee
roaster through multiple test roasts.

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:46.070
After heating the roast chamber to a
specific temperature, the operator uses the

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:48.020
touch screen to release the beans into the
chamber.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:54.110
The hot air travels from the cyclone to the
roast chamber, then through a return pipe

00:08:54.110 --> 00:09:01.130
back to the cyclone where it's cleaned and
sent back to the chamber to know when to

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:02.990
manually end the roast cycle.

00:09:03.110 --> 00:09:06.650
The operator views smells and listens to the
beans.

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:12.740
That's because coffee beans make crackling
noises as they expand and shed their chaff.

00:09:14.890 --> 00:09:19.900
The machine can also be set to end the row
cycle automatically when the beans reach

00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:21.280
their target temperature.

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:24.730
A trapdoor opens to draw in ambient air.

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:30.070
This cooler air pushes smoke in the roasting
chamber up to the cyclone for incineration.

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:35.410
The computer can automatically open the
roast chambers discharge door or the operator

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:36.850
can do so manually.

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:40.900
As soon as the roasted coffee beans drop
into the cooling tray.

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:45.880
The stirring arms and cooling fan
automatically start up if the beans are

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:47.050
perfectly roasted.

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:50.590
The machine is ready to be shipped to a
coffee roasting business.

